title: Conditional Covenants and the Forfeited Promises of Ezekiel’s Temple
description: A book-by-book analysis of God’s merciful winnowing: unconditional and conditional promises, human choices and changed outcomes, from Cain to Ezekiel’s temple and the “new nation” of believers.
output: lessons/prophecy/conditional-covenants-ezekiel-temple.html

# Built from studies/data/notes/conditional-covenants-ezekiel-temple-notes.html, book by book.
# Each book keeps the notes' three parts: Promises and Conditions, Choices and Outcomes, Changed Promises.
# The verses the notes quote inline are shown from the KJV (scripture: blocks or verse buttons), never typed.

=== Introduction
--- Conditional Covenants and the Forfeited Promises of Ezekiel’s Temple [title]
overline: Prophecy & the covenants
heading: Conditional Covenants and the Forfeited Promises of Ezekiel’s Temple
sub: A Book-by-Book Analysis of God’s Merciful Winnowing: Promises, Conditions, Choices, and Changed Outcomes
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- God’s Merciful Winnowing
lead: The Bible reveals God’s redemptive plan through a dynamic interplay of unconditional promises, fulfilled in Christ, and conditional promises, contingent on human obedience, within a merciful winnowing process rooted in His love.
panel: Individual | Judgment by faith. [[Romans 4:3]]
panel-warn: National | Judgment by works. [[Obadiah 1:10|e.g., Obadiah 1:10]]
p: This framework distinguishes individual judgment by faith from national judgment by works, emphasizing God’s longsuffering, free will, and clear presentation of choices between life and death.
big: Deuteronomy 30:19 | mark: I have set before you life and death, blessing and cursing: therefore choose life

--- Foreknowledge and the “New Nation”
p: God’s foreknowledge ([[Isaiah 46:9-10]]) enables Him to offer conditional promises, respecting human choices, while adapting His plan when conditions are unmet, redirecting blessings to others, such as the “new nation” of believers ([[1 Peter 2:9]]).
p: This article applies this lens in a book-by-book analysis, detailing examples of promises, conditions, human choices, and changed outcomes, expanding on cases like Saul’s loss of a dynasty and Jesus’ lineage, and incorporating additional instances to develop the theory comprehensively.
image: lessons/prophecy/conditional-covenants-ezekiel-temple-pattern.svg | The pattern in every book: Promises and Conditions, then Choices and Outcomes, then Changed Promises; when conditions are unmet, blessings are redirected to others, such as the new nation of believers | The pattern traced in every book

--- Unconditional and Conditional
kicker: The promises at a glance
table: Promise | Unconditional or conditional
tr: A Redeemer [[Genesis 3:15]] | **Unconditional**, fulfilled in Christ
tr: Abraham’s Covenant [[Genesis 12:2-3]] | The salvation aspect is **unconditional** [[Galatians 3:16]]; the land’s immediate possession **requires obedience**
tr: Deliverance from Egypt and Canaan [[Exodus 6:8]] | The condition is **covenantal obedience** [[Exodus 19:5]]
tr: Saul’s dynasty [[1 Samuel 13:13]] | The condition is **obedience**
tr: The Davidic Covenant [[2 Samuel 7:16]] | **Unconditional** in Christ but **conditional** for immediate blessings
tr: Jeroboam’s dynasty [[1 Kings 11:38]] | The condition is **obedience**
tr: Ezekiel’s temple and land [[Ezekiel 43:10-11]] | **Contingent on repentance**
tr: The New Covenant [[Hebrews 8:13]] | **Unconditional** in Christ’s priesthood [[Hebrews 7:24-25]]
tr: The millennial kingdom [[Revelation 20:4-6]] | **Unconditional** for believers but **conditional** for nations [[Revelation 20:7-9]]

=== The Law
--- The Foundation of Winnowing
kicker: Genesis
p: Genesis establishes God’s pattern of offering conditional promises alongside human free will. After the Fall, God promises a Redeemer ([[Genesis 3:15]]), an unconditional promise fulfilled in Christ. However, other promises are conditional:
panel: Cain’s Offering | God promises acceptance if Cain obeys ([[Genesis 4:7]]). The condition is a right sacrifice, reflecting faith and obedience.
panel: Abraham’s Covenant | God promises a great nation and land ([[Genesis 12:2-3]]). While the salvation aspect is unconditional ([[Galatians 3:16]]), the land’s immediate possession requires obedience.
scripture: Genesis 4:7

--- Genesis: Choices and Outcomes
kicker: Genesis
step: Cain’s Rejection | Cain chooses disobedience, offering improperly and murdering Abel ([[Genesis 4:5-8]]). God’s longsuffering offers a second chance, but Cain’s refusal leads to exile ([[Genesis 4:12]]), winnowing him from the faithful line through Seth.
step: Abraham’s Delay | Abraham’s lack of faith, deceiving Pharaoh in Egypt ([[Genesis 12:10-13]]), delays the promise’s fulfillment ([[Genesis 12:17]]). God mercifully restores him, but the choice sets a precedent for Israel’s later failures.
step: Lot’s Choice | Lot chooses the plain of Jordan, near Sodom ([[Genesis 13:10-11]]), prioritizing wealth over righteousness. His proximity to sin leads to captivity ([[Genesis 14:12]]) and eventual loss ([[Genesis 19:30-38]]), winnowing him from Abraham’s blessed line.
callout: **Changed Promises:** Cain’s forfeiture shifts the godly line to Seth ([[Genesis 4:25]]). Abraham’s delay does not nullify the covenant but postpones its realization, requiring further obedience ([[Genesis 15:7]]). Lot’s descendants, Moab and Ammon ([[Genesis 19:37-38]]), receive no covenantal blessing, illustrating God’s redirection.

--- National Promises and Individual Choices
kicker: Exodus
p: God promises to deliver Israel from Egypt and give them Canaan ([[Exodus 6:8]]). The condition is covenantal obedience:
big: Exodus 19:5 | mark: if ye will obey my voice indeed, and keep my covenant
panel: Moses’ Leadership | God promises Moses the privilege of leading Israel into Canaan ([[Exodus 3:8]]). The condition is unwavering obedience.
panel: Pharaoh’s Opportunity | God offers Pharaoh multiple chances to release Israel, with warnings of plagues ([[Exodus 7:16]]).

--- Exodus: Choices and Outcomes
kicker: Exodus
step: Pharaoh’s Defiance | Pharaoh’s ten refusals (Exodus 7:13-12:30) harden his heart ([[Exodus 9:12]]). God’s longsuffering delays judgment, but Pharaoh’s choices lead to Egypt’s destruction ([[Exodus 12:29-30]]), winnowing Egypt from God’s plan.
step: Israel’s Murmuring | Israel’s unbelief at the Red Sea ([[Exodus 14:11-12]]) and complaints ([[Exodus 16:3]]) test God’s patience. His mercy sustains them, but sets the stage for later forfeiture.
step: Moses’ Disobedience | Moses strikes the rock twice, disobeying God’s command to speak ([[Numbers 20:11-12]]). He forfeits entering Canaan.
callout: **Changed Promises:** Pharaoh’s rejection elevates Israel as God’s chosen nation. Israel’s failures delay the promise, foreshadowing Kadeshbarnea. Moses’ disqualification shifts leadership to Joshua ([[Deuteronomy 34:9]]), illustrating personal winnowing within God’s plan.

--- Priesthood and National Holiness
kicker: Leviticus
p: God promises Israel a holy status if they obey His laws ([[Leviticus 20:26]]). The priesthood is conditionally offered to the firstborn, later specified to Levi ([[Numbers 3:12-13]]), based on faithfulness.
step: Golden Calf | Israel’s idolatry ([[Exodus 32:1-6]]) violates the covenant. The Levites’ loyalty ([[Exodus 32:26-29]]) wins them the priesthood, winnowing them from other tribes.
step: Nadab and Abihu | Aaron’s sons offer “strange fire” ([[Leviticus 10:1-2]]), disobeying God’s commands. Their death forfeits their priestly role, redirecting it to Eleazar and Ithamar.
callout: **Changed Promises:** The priesthood shifts from the firstborn to Levi ([[Numbers 3:12]]), a national winnowing. Nadab and Abihu’s forfeiture narrows the priesthood within Levi, illustrating God’s precision in judging works.

--- Forfeiture of the Promised Land
kicker: Numbers
p: God reaffirms the land promise ([[Numbers 13:2]]), conditioned on trust and obedience. The spies’ mission tests Israel’s faith.
step: Kadeshbarnea Rebellion | Ten spies’ unbelief ([[Numbers 13:31-33]]) incites rebellion ([[Numbers 14:1-4]]). God declares:
scripture: Numbers 14:22-23
p: That generation dies in the wilderness ([[Hebrews 3:17-19]]).
step: Korah’s Rebellion | Korah challenges Moses’ leadership ([[Numbers 16:3]]), seeking priestly authority. His destruction ([[Numbers 16:31-35]]) winnows him from God’s plan.
callout: **Changed Promises:** The land promise is delayed to the next generation under Joshua ([[Numbers 14:31]]). Korah’s line is excluded from priesthood, reinforcing Levi’s role.

--- Clear Choices and National Judgment
kicker: Deuteronomy
p: God outlines blessings for obedience and curses for disobedience ([[Deuteronomy 28:1-68]]). [[Deuteronomy 30:19]] summarizes:
big: Deuteronomy 30:19 | mark: I have set before you life and death, blessing and cursing: therefore choose life
step: Covenant Renewal | Israel recommits to obedience ([[Deuteronomy 29:12-13]]), but God foreknows their rebellion ([[Deuteronomy 31:16]]). Their later idolatry triggers curses ([[2 Kings 17:7-23]]).
step: Moses’ Final Warning | Moses’ exclusion from Canaan ([[Deuteronomy 34:4-5]]) serves as a warning to Israel.
callout: **Changed Promises:** The promise persists for future generations, but national disobedience leads to exile, redirecting blessings to a remnant ([[Deuteronomy 30:1-5]]).

=== The land and the kings
--- Fulfillment and Continued Conditions
kicker: Joshua
p: The land promise is fulfilled. Continued possession requires obedience ([[Joshua 23:6]]).
scripture: Joshua 21:43-45 | mark: There failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken unto the house of Israel; all came to pass
step: Achan’s Sin | Achan’s theft ([[Joshua 7:1]]) violates God’s command, causing defeat at Ai ([[Joshua 7:4-5]]). His death ([[Joshua 7:25]]) restores blessing, winnowing him from Israel.
step: Tribes’ Negligence | Failure to fully expel Canaanites ([[Joshua 16:10]]) leads to future idolatry ([[Judges 2:1-3]]).
callout: **Changed Promises:** The land is secured, but incomplete obedience plants seeds for future judgment, redirecting full blessing to a faithful remnant.

--- Cycles of Disobedience and Mercy
kicker: Judges
p: God promises deliverance if Israel repents ([[Judges 2:16]]). Obedience is the condition for peace.
step: Idolatry Cycles | Israel’s repeated idolatry ([[Judges 2:11-13]]) leads to oppression. God’s longsuffering raises judges (e.g., Gideon, [[Judges 6:11-14]]), but disobedience persists.
step: Samson’s Failure | Samson’s promise as a deliverer ([[Judges 13:5]]) is conditional on Nazirite vows. His violations ([[Judges 14:8-9]]; [[Judges 16:17|16:17]]) lead to captivity, limiting his impact.
callout: **Changed Promises:** Deliverance is temporary, redirecting God’s plan to a monarchy ([[Judges 17:6]]).

--- Saul’s Forfeited Lineage
kicker: 1 Samuel
p: God promises Saul a lasting dynasty. The condition is obedience, with the potential for Saul’s line to include the Messiah.
big: 1 Samuel 13:13 | mark: would the LORD have established thy kingdom upon Israel for ever
step: Saul’s Disobedience | Saul’s unlawful sacrifice ([[1 Samuel 13:8-13]]) and sparing Amalek ([[1 Samuel 15:9]]) violate God’s commands. God rejects him ([[1 Samuel 15:26]]). He forfeits not only a dynasty but the honor of being in Jesus’ lineage ([[Matthew 1:1-6]], tracing through David).
step: Michal’s Barrenness | Saul’s daughter Michal, married to David ([[1 Samuel 18:27]]), could have linked Saul’s line to the Messiah. Her disdain for David’s worship ([[2 Samuel 6:16]], [[2 Samuel 6:23|23]]) ends this opportunity.
callout: **Changed Promises:** The Messianic lineage shifts to David ([[1 Samuel 16:13]]), fulfilling the Davidic Covenant ([[2 Samuel 7:16]]).

--- Saul and Michal
kicker: 1 Samuel
scripture: 1 Samuel 15:26
scripture: 2 Samuel 6:23

--- David’s Forfeited Temple
kicker: 2 Samuel and 1 Chronicles
p: God permits David to plan the temple ([[1 Chronicles 28:2-3]]), conditioned on personal holiness. The Davidic Covenant promises an eternal throne ([[2 Samuel 7:16]]), unconditional in Christ but conditional for immediate blessings.
step: David’s Bloodshed | David’s wars disqualify him ([[1 Chronicles 22:8]]). He forfeits building the temple.
step: Adultery and Murder | David’s sin with Bathsheba ([[2 Samuel 11:2-17]]) brings judgment ([[2 Samuel 12:10-12]]), delaying kingdom stability.
callout: **Changed Promises:** Solomon builds the temple ([[1 Kings 6:1]]), and the Messianic promise persists through David’s line ([[Luke 1:32-33]]).

--- Jeroboam’s Lost Dynasty
kicker: 1 Kings
p: God promises Jeroboam a dynasty like David’s. The condition is obedience.
big: 1 Kings 11:38 | mark: build thee a sure house, as I built for David
step: Jeroboam’s Idolatry | Jeroboam erects golden calves ([[1 Kings 12:28-30]]), leading Israel into sin. God declares ([[1 Kings 14:10]]), ending his line:
scripture: 1 Kings 14:10 | mark: I will bring evil upon the house of Jeroboam
callout: **Changed Promises:** The promise shifts to Judah’s line, preserving David’s dynasty ([[1 Kings 11:36]]).

--- Northern Kingdom’s Exile
kicker: 2 Kings
p: God promises protection if Israel obeys ([[Deuteronomy 28:1-14]]). Idolatry violates this ([[2 Kings 17:7-8]]).
step: Persistent Idolatry | The Northern Kingdom’s sins lead to exile ([[2 Kings 17:23]]). God’s longsuffering sends prophets, but rejection prevails.
callout: **Changed Promises:** Blessings redirect to Judah, then to a remnant ([[2 Kings 19:30-31]]).

=== The prophets
--- Judgment of Nations by Works
kicker: Isaiah
p: God promises judgment on nations based on works ([[Isaiah 13:11]]). Israel’s restoration is conditional:
big: Isaiah 1:19
step: Babylon’s Pride | Babylon’s arrogance ([[Isaiah 14:13-14]]) leads to destruction ([[Isaiah 14:22]]).
step: Israel’s Rebellion | Disobedience brings exile ([[Isaiah 39:6-7]]), but God’s mercy offers restoration ([[Isaiah 40:1-2]]).
callout: **Changed Promises:** A remnant returns ([[Isaiah 10:20-22]]), foreshadowing the “new nation” ([[1 Peter 2:9]]).

--- Reprobate Silver
kicker: Jeremiah
p: God promises restoration if Judah repents:
big: Jeremiah 7:3
step: Judah’s Rejection | Persistent idolatry ([[Jeremiah 7:18]]) renders them “reprobate silver” ([[Jeremiah 6:30]]).
scripture: Jeremiah 6:30
callout: **Changed Promises:** A remnant is preserved ([[Jeremiah 29:11]]), redirecting to the New Covenant ([[Jeremiah 31:31-34]]).

--- Forfeited Temple and Land
kicker: Ezekiel
p: Ezekiel 40-48 promises a restored temple and land, contingent on repentance. The land allotment is conditional ([[Ezekiel 45:1-8]]).
scripture: Ezekiel 43:10-11 | mark: that they may be ashamed of their iniquities; if they be ashamed of all that they have done
step: Israel’s Rebellion | Failure to repent ([[Ezekiel 2:3-5]]) forfeits the vision, rendering it “what could have been.”
step: Gog and Magog | Ezekiel 38-39’s battle, conditional on Israel’s obedience, shifts to [[Revelation 20:7-9]] for the “new nation.”
callout: **Changed Promises:** The temple’s role is fulfilled spiritually ([[1 Corinthians 3:16]]), and land promises redirect to believers ([[Romans 11:17-24]]).

--- Edom’s Judgment by Works
kicker: Obadiah
p: Edom faces judgment for violence:
big: Obadiah 1:10
step: Edom’s Pride | Pride and betrayal ([[Obadiah 1:3-4]]) lead to destruction.
callout: **Changed Promises:** Israel’s remnant inherits Edom’s land ([[Obadiah 1:17]]), redirecting blessings.

--- Nineveh’s Temporary Repentance
kicker: Jonah
p: God promises Nineveh’s destruction unless they repent:
big: Jonah 3:4
step: Nineveh’s Repentance | They repent ([[Jonah 3:5-10]]), sparing the city temporarily. Later relapse ([[Nahum 3:1-7]]) brings judgment.
callout: **Changed Promises:** God’s mercy delays judgment, but Nineveh’s works determine their fate.

--- Conditional Eschatology
kicker: Zechariah
p: [[Zechariah 6:12-15]] promises a kingdom “if ye will diligently obey.” [[Zechariah 14:16-19]] ties worship to blessing, with judgment for noncompliance.
scripture: Zechariah 6:15 | mark: if ye will diligently obey
step: Israel’s Rejection | Failure to obey ([[Zechariah 7:11-12]]) forfeits these promises.
callout: **Changed Promises:** Blessings redirect to the “new nation” ([[Revelation 20:4-6]]).

=== The New Testament
--- Kingdom Offer Rejected
kicker: Matthew
p: Jesus offers the kingdom, conditioned on repentance.
big: Matthew 4:17
step: Pharisees’ Blasphemy | Accusing Jesus of demonic power ([[Matthew 12:24]]) hardens hearts.
step: Crucifixion | The cry ([[Matthew 27:25]]) invokes judgment, despite Jesus’ prayer ([[Luke 23:34]]).
scripture: Matthew 27:25
callout: **Changed Promises:** The kingdom is given to a “new nation” ([[Matthew 21:43]]).

--- Final Rejection
kicker: Acts
p: Peter offers restoration ([[Acts 3:19-21]]).
scripture: Acts 3:19
step: Stephen’s Martyrdom | Israel’s rejection ([[Acts 7:51]]) finalizes their winnowing ([[Acts 7:58]]).
scripture: Acts 7:51
callout: **Changed Promises:** The gospel goes to the Gentiles ([[Acts 13:46]]), forming the “new nation” ([[Romans 11:17-24]]).

--- New Covenant Nation
kicker: Romans
p: [[Romans 11:17-24]] describes Gentiles grafted into Israel’s promises, conditioned on faith ([[Romans 11:20]]).
step: Israel’s Unbelief | Their rejection ([[Romans 11:15]]) opens salvation to Gentiles.
step: Gentile Faith | Acceptance by faith ([[Romans 11:23]]) secures blessings.
callout: **Changed Promises:** The “new nation” inherits spiritual promises ([[Galatians 3:28-29]]).

--- Superior Covenant
kicker: Hebrews
p: The New Covenant replaces the old, unconditional in Christ’s priesthood ([[Hebrews 7:24-25]]).
big: Hebrews 8:13
step: Israel’s Failure | Rejection of Christ ([[Hebrews 3:12]]) forfeits OT promises.
step: Believers’ Faith | Acceptance fulfills spiritual roles ([[Hebrews 10:10]]).
callout: **Changed Promises:** Christ’s sacrifice ends OT ordinances, redirecting to believers ([[1 Peter 2:5]]).

--- Millennial Fulfillment
kicker: Revelation
p: A millennial kingdom is promised ([[Revelation 20:4-6]]), unconditional for believers but conditional for nations ([[Revelation 20:7-9]]).
step: Satan’s Rebellion | Nations’ rebellion ([[Revelation 20:8]]) leads to judgment.
step: Believers’ Faith | The faithful reign with Christ ([[Revelation 20:4]]).
callout: **Changed Promises:** The eternal state follows ([[Revelation 21:1]]), fulfilling all promises in Christ.

=== Conclusion
--- Forfeited and Redirected
kicker: The winnowing, book by book
table: Forfeited | Redirected to
tr: Cain [[Genesis 4:25]] | The godly line through Seth
tr: The firstborn [[Numbers 3:12]] | The priesthood to Levi
tr: Nadab and Abihu [[Leviticus 10:1-2]] | Eleazar and Ithamar
tr: Moses [[Deuteronomy 34:9]] | Leadership to Joshua
tr: That generation [[Numbers 14:31]] | The next generation under Joshua
tr: Saul [[1 Samuel 16:13]] | The Messianic lineage to David
tr: David, building the temple [[1 Kings 6:1]] | Solomon builds the temple
tr: Jeroboam [[1 Kings 11:36]] | Judah’s line, preserving David’s dynasty
tr: The Northern Kingdom [[2 Kings 19:30-31]] | Judah, then a remnant
tr: Edom [[Obadiah 1:17]] | Israel’s remnant inherits Edom’s land
tr: Israel [[Matthew 21:43]] | The kingdom is given to a “new nation”

--- Conclusion
p: This book-by-book analysis demonstrates God’s merciful winnowing, offering conditional promises, respecting free will, and adapting His plan when conditions are unmet.
p: From Cain’s rejection to Israel’s forfeiture of Ezekiel’s Temple, God’s love provides clear choices ([[Deuteronomy 30:19]]), repeated opportunities ([[2 Peter 3:9]]), and judgments based on faith for individuals and works for nations.
p: The fulfilled land promise ([[Joshua 21:43-45]]) and redirected blessings to the “new nation” ([[1 Peter 2:9]]) refute alternative views, affirming God’s sovereignty ([[Isaiah 46:10]]) and desire for all to choose life.
